 her  constituents when they came time to help  my people they left us alone they left  this baloney headlines of people  Assemblywoman Catalina Cruz who is an  angel in her neighborhood Maria Elena  Salinas will anchor this special airing  Sunday she's with us from Miami Maria  Elena good morning why are Latino  communities especially vulnerable to  this virus good morning Anthony good to  talk to you but there are several  reasons one of them of course is a  medical factor Latinos are more prone to  get diabetes and heart disease there's  also the cultural factor which is many  multi-generational families are Latino  they live in small spaces that sometimes  prevents them from being able to social  distance but the main factor is that  they are essential workers that are out  there keeping the economy going and  they're doing jobs that cannot be done  from home and that's probably one of the  things that exposes the Latino community  more to this virus so what can  government officials do to help well  we're not seeing much help from the  federal government or from state  governments with the possible exception  of California and that's why you see  people local politicians just like  Catalina Cruz as you saw in the store  stepping up and helping their community  and what we have seen is that National  Hispanic civic organizations activists  advocates philanthropists have really  stepped up and the Hispanic community to  help the user platforms to advocate but  they also you know reach into their  pockets to be able to create some kind  of funds for for the Latino community  Elena does does immigration status  ultimately affect whether someone can  get a test or whether they're actually  going to seek treatment but  unfortunately it does it does prevent  them not having the necessary documents  legal documents immigration documents  it's really difficult because access to  health care or to adequate health care  is more complicated one of the reasons I  think also